<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#juren">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juren</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as recommended man</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juren, literally recommended man, was the rank won by passing the provincial examination, the xiangshi, in the imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>: above the shengyuan and below the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">jinshi</a>, the highest degree. In the Ming and Qing dynasties candidates who already held the shengyuan rank sat the examination every three years in their provincial capital, under strict rules against favour; quotas kept success rare, and by 1630 about 49,200 candidates competed for 1,279 degrees. Women, clergy and merchants were excluded. The rank could also be bought, and it brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> status and its privileges. The title ended with the abolition of the examinations in 1904.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juren.jpg" alt="Juren" width="330" height="497" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 叶衍兰 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wei_Yuan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word juren was first used under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> for men at the provincial level who were recommended for civil service; they still had to pass an examination of the central government before receiving an official title. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a> was formally established under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>, juren under the Sui, Tang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> meant a candidate in the state examinations. Only under the Ming and Qing did it become the title of those who had passed the provincial examination. A military juren, or wu juren, was also created when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wu-zetian">Wu Zetian</a> introduced military examinations, which tested archery, horsemanship and the handling of polearms besides texts such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-tzu">Sun Tzu</a>'s The Art of War.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Under the Ming and Qing the xiangshi was held in the autumn of every third year. Candidates had to sit it in their own registered province, in three written sessions over a week, each separated by days of rest. An Imperial Commissioner came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to oversee it, his residence guarded against candidates and their families. Essays were copied in red ink so that examiners could not recognise handwriting, names were concealed, and as many as eight examiners might grade one paper. Candidates wrote in cells lining long alleys, with up to ten or twelve thousand people in one hall, and the one who came first was titled jieyuan.</p><p>Under the Qing the sessions tested the Four Books and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Five Classics</a>, the Classic of Filial Piety, verdicts and official documents, and questions on history and administration, all in the rigid eight-legged essay form. The syllabus was revised many times until it was fixed after 1793. Purchase, known as juanna, was legal and overseen by the government, and bought degrees carried the same benefits as earned ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Passing qualified a man for lower official posts. In the early and middle Ming juren served as education officials at prefecture, county and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> level; in the late Ming many became county magistrates, responsible for taxes and order, though by the Qing jinshi holders were displacing them from higher posts. Only juren could sit the national examination for the jinshi.</p><p>The title was held for life but could not be inherited. Commoners addressed juren as laoye, your honour, and their families could raise flagpoles with red and gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> flags as flagpole families. They were exempt from labour service and corporal punishment, could not be arrested without a special imperial order, and enjoyed tax reductions and the right to wear a scholar's robe.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juren” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
